-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('640cdf0e-ec79-5269-8460-e6e48ea6f1a2', 'rg12-theme-summary-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Second Notebook$q$, $q$In the spring of 1884, sixteen-year-old Wendell Ashby was hired to carry the measuring chain for a government surveyor named Cyrus Holt, who had been sent to map the Larkin Valley for a new county atlas. The work was measured in steps and angles. Every evening Holt copied the day's figures into a leather field book, and every evening he assigned names to the creeks and hills they had crossed. A stream became Holt Creek. A ridge became Commissioner's Ridge, after the official who had approved the survey.

Wendell noticed that the people who lived in the valley used other names. The widow who sold them eggs called the stream Two Kettles, because her grandparents had once found two iron kettles in its bank after a flood. The farmers at the crossroads called the ridge the Long Shade, since it put their fields in shadow by four in the afternoon. A schoolteacher named Ana Brodsky could explain the origin of nearly every name in the valley, and she did, at length, whenever Wendell stopped to fill his canteen at the school pump.

He began keeping a second notebook, a cheap one bought at the general store, where he wrote down each local name and the story behind it. He did not show it to Holt. It seemed like a hobby, not work.

In August Holt caught a fever and spent a week in bed at the boarding house. Wendell finished the eastern section alone, and when Holt reviewed the field book, he found that the boy had labeled every feature with two names: the official one above the line and the local one below. Holt frowned for a long time. Then he asked to see the second notebook.

The atlas, when it was printed two years later, used Holt's names on its maps. But in its appendix, under a heading Holt wrote himself, "Names in Common Use," it listed Two Kettles, the Long Shade, and forty others, each with a sentence of history credited to the residents of Larkin Valley.$q$, 'draft')
