   $q$How does the line "The strongest grip in the world did nothing in a drawer" mark a turning point in the theme's development?$q$, $q$[{"text":"It shows Ji-ho realizing that a feature's value depends on whether the person actually uses the device.","is_correct":true},{"text":"It shows Ji-ho deciding that grip strength does not matter at all in a prosthetic.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstates-claim"},{"text":"It shows Ji-ho blaming Amara's family for storing the hand carelessly.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-character"},{"text":"It shows that Amara has lost the hand and Ji-ho must print a replacement.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$It shows Ji-ho realizing that a feature's value depends on whether the person actually uses the device.$q$, $q$Ji-ho moves from treating Amara's requests as "trivial" to seeing that an unworn hand has no real strength. He still values grip but accepts losing "some of the extra grip," so he does not dismiss strength entirely.$q$, 'draft', '94c40bda-aadc-5df1-9bef-f79653aa3c85')
