-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e3c23a21-e91e-5e9a-bdfa-606154d2af13', 'rg12-word-parts-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Bay That Glows$q$, $q$On moonless summer nights, kayakers paddle into a small, sheltered bay on a tropical coast and watch the water ignite. Every stroke of a paddle leaves a swirl of blue-green light, and fish darting below the surface trace glowing streaks like sparks. The source of this display is a single-celled organism called a dinoflagellate, a creature so small that one cupful of bay water can hold thousands of them.

Each organism is bioluminescent, producing its own light through a chemical reaction inside its cell. When the water is disturbed, the reaction releases a brief flash. In daylight the organisms are nearly invisible, and their translucent bodies let sunlight pass straight through them. Only in darkness does their collective glow become obvious.

For years, visitors offered all sorts of theories about why the bay shone so brightly. Marine biologists eventually helped elucidate the mystery. The bay's narrow mouth keeps the organisms from washing out to sea, while mangrove trees along the shore drop leaves that enrich the water with nutrients. The result is a crowded, well-fed population.

The glow is also fragile. Researchers have recorded nights when the light dimmed to an almost imperceptible shimmer, often after heavy rains diluted the bay or after boat engines churned up sediment. Local guides now limit the number of tours and ask visitors to keep sunscreen out of the water.

Protecting the bay requires steady attention, but the effort pays off. A healthy bay keeps its vitality season after season, and each new group of paddlers gets to see the ocean turn to light.$q$, 'draft')
