insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('522d847e-ee97-58b3-95a7-1ab8533b8011', 'rg12-word-parts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Messages From Deep Space$q$, $q$When a spacecraft sends data home from billions of miles away, its signal arrives unimaginably faint. Along the way, stray radiation and electrical noise can flip a one into a zero or a zero into a one. A single corrupted bit might turn a crisp photograph of a distant moon into a streaked, unreadable image, or turn a temperature reading into nonsense.

Engineers cannot simply ask a distant probe to repeat itself, because each round trip of a signal may take hours. Instead, they rely on error-correcting codes. Before sending, the spacecraft adds carefully calculated extra bits to every block of data. When the block arrives on Earth, computers check whether those extra bits still fit the expected pattern. If they do not, the computers can often locate the erroneous bit and flip it back without requesting the message again.

The same idea protects the telemetry that keeps mission teams informed about a craft's health: its fuel level, its battery charge, and the temperature of its instruments. If a reading seems erratic, jumping wildly from one moment to the next, engineers must decide whether the instrument is truly failing or whether the signal was simply damaged on its journey.

Occasionally, a strange value turns out to be an aberration caused by a high-energy particle striking a memory chip, and the problem disappears on its own. Other times, the odd numbers are the first warning of a real fault. Telling the difference requires patience, careful records, and a healthy suspicion of any single number.$q$, 'draft')
